 {"id":"QA-SPECLANG-008","status":"pending","priority":"P2","type":"bug","complexity":2,"title":"[P2] ui-probe drives 'npm run dev -- --port', which for speclang is tsc --watch (TS5023): UI surface never probed, reported FAIL instead of UNVERIFIED","reasoning":"speclang-qa-2026-11-16-06-03-37 cell=ui-probe. The rig launched 'npm run dev -- --port 3111'; speclang's dev script is 'tsc --watch', so npm appended '--port 3111' to tsc and the stage died with 'error TS5023: Unknown compiler option --port' (ui.log), http=000. The repo does ship a UI: scripts.dev:dashboard = 'vite dev' with vite ^8.1.5 installed (node_modules/.bin/vite present in a fresh clone). So the UI surface was never actually probed and its status is UNVERIFIED, not FAIL. Battery evidence: /home/kara/.hermes/cache/scratch/speclang-qa-2026-11-16-06-03-37/harvest/qa-evidence.jsonl, SHA256 b516fb98a6307c3ea14c813862b7c12896fb5c0e68427049c5716a29e00f6604.","acceptance_criteria":"bunker-qa.sh ui-probe must select a script that actually serves HTTP (for speclang that is dev:dashboard / vite) or detect that the chosen script is not a server and report the UI surface UNVERIFIED with the selected script named. Verify by re-running the speclang battery: ui-probe must either land an HTTP response from the vite dashboard (non-000) or report UNVERIFIED, and must never emit a TS5023 compiler-option error as a product FAIL.","detail":{"metadata":{},"fingerprint":"bc5e4a57af02d54a99d1aae80f357001ebc957e63ccbe064e269cdb0847e45f6"},"capability_tags":[],"created_at":"2026-11-16T07:02:44.749297+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-16T07:02:44.749297+00:00"}
 {"id":"README-21","status":"pending","priority":"P4","type":"task","complexity":3,"title":"P4 \u2014 README:183-194 'Example tree from project.scl' is fabricated: project.scl declares neither @ref:speclang/auth nor @ref:speclang/database, and 3 of the block's 5 leaf paths do not exist","reasoning":"README health sweep 2026-11-16 (speclang-readme lane). Doc severity P4 per Bane ruling 2026-09-30 (policy, not an assessment of the finding's class).\n\nFINDING (accuracy \u2014 a fenced tree presented as project.scl's real structure):\nREADME.md:183 says 'Example tree from `project.scl` (Layer 0):' and is followed by a fenced block (README.md:184-194) claiming this shape:\n  project.scl -> @ref:speclang/core -> { @ref:speclang/auth -> specs/auth/implementation.spec.md, src/auth/handler.ts }\n                                   -> { @ref:speclang/database -> src/db/client.ts }\n  project.scl -> @ref:speclang/cascade -> specs/cascade-protocol.spec.md\n\nMeasured against the repo at HEAD (/home/kara/speclang, sweep of 2026-11-16):\n\n1. specs/project.scl declares id \"@northstar/speclang\" (line 2), layer 0 (line 4), and 24 @ref: entries at lines 124-146: core, daemon, agent-protocol, skills, pipeline, recovery, file-naming, test-specs, cascade, workflow, opencode, headers, directory-structure, tools, sqlite, dynamic-split, deployment, mcp, mcp-openapi-generation, mcp-openapi-generation-cli, mcp-daemon, git-history, symlinks.\n   $ grep -c \"@ref:\" specs/project.scl          -> 24\n   $ grep -nE \"@ref:speclang/(auth|database)\" specs/project.scl -> no matches\n   So two of the block's three @refs (speclang/auth, speclang/database) are NOT refs of project.scl, and 22 of its 23 real refs never appear in the \"example tree\".\n\n2. Three of the five leaf paths in the block do not exist:\n   $ for p in specs/auth/implementation.spec.md src/auth/handler.ts src/db/client.ts; do test -e \"$p\" || echo MISSING $p; done\n     MISSING specs/auth/implementation.spec.md\n     MISSING src/auth/handler.ts\n     MISSING src/db/client.ts\n   $ ls -d src/auth            -> ls: cannot access 'src/auth': No such file or directory\n   $ ls -d specs/auth*         -> specs/auth.spec.dir   specs/auth.spec.md    (no implementation.spec.md)\n   $ ls -la src/db             -> only symlinks index.ts, migrations.ts, poc-db.ts; no client.ts\n   The two paths that DO exist: specs/core.spec.md and specs/cascade-protocol.spec.md.\n\n3. Consequence: the section is titled 'Specs Define the Tree Structure' and the reader learns the layer model from a tree no file in the repo produces \u2014 the two example branches a newcomer is most likely to try (auth, database) lead to files that have never existed.\n\nRelated, deliberately NOT duplicated here: README-5 (README.md:348/:533 examples drift), README-9 (README.md:412-414 compile-health claims that mention src/auth), README-19 (spec counts). This row is scoped to README.md:183-194 only.\n\nFIX: replace the fenced block with a tree derived from project.scl's actual refs (or a small real subtree whose entries all resolve), and drop the auth/database/client.ts branch unless those files are created \u2014 or create the files the docs promise. Do not invent refs.\n\nPASS: 'sed -n \"184,194p\" README.md' piped through a per-path existence test prints no MISSING line, and any @ref: shown inside the block is present in specs/project.scl (grep -c for each >= 1); equivalently the block is replaced and 'grep -cE \"@ref:speclang/(auth|database)\" specs/project.scl' matches what the block shows.","acceptance_criteria":"The 'Example tree from project.scl' block is reconciled to output a reader can reproduce: the documented command that lists the project's spec tree from specs/project.scl exits 0, and every leaf path shown in the README block appears verbatim in that command's output (zero README leaves missing from it). Every `@ref:` identifier the block depicts is declared in specs/project.scl (grep -c on the spec file is >= 1 for each). If the tree cannot be produced from project.scl at all, the block is replaced by the honest generated output plus the exact command. Evidence: the command, its exit code, and the diff between block leaves and generated leaves.","detail":{"metadata":{},"fingerprint":"6c97744ad95f6723bf6810058ae106bcb8870602ea14ac47ecdbf74f5ff11ede"},"capability_tags":["docs","readme"],"created_at":"2026-11-16T10:58:06.437399+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-17T10:22:37.678118+00:00"}
-{"id":"RELEASE-002","status":"pending","priority":"P1","type":"bug","complexity":2,"title":"P1 [releng] remote tags v0.1.0 and v1.0.0 point at commits unreachable from main (pi-agent-migration / speclang-selfhosting)","reasoning":"From git ls-remote origin: v0.1.0 peels to 676795fcfb25df99c97dae6f204774cf8a3c5e3f and v1.0.0 peels to 2f2639a87a2615d6c5e232c83943ac6c871385e0. Neither satisfies git merge-base --is-ancestor against the release-authority tip b9bb5042; git branch -a --contains 2f2639a8 shows only origin/pi-agent-migration and origin/speclang-selfhosting. The local tags under the same names peel to different commits (v0.1.0 -> ff96a2e9, v1.0.0 -> f7b4ce7d) that ARE ancestors of main, so local and remote tag identity disagree by construction. Consequence for release work: a consumer who checks out v1.0.0 from GitHub receives code that was never merged into main, the stale lineages carry 992 and 964 commits not reachable from main, and no main-lineage baseline exists for a commit census except the local peel f7b4ce7d. This blocks the release baseline definition independently of code readiness. Doctrine forbids force-moving an existing tag, so the repair is a documented supersession on the main lineage rather than a re-point. Secret scans over origin/main..master, origin/main..speclang-selfhosting and origin/main..pi-agent-migration with gitleaks (redacted) returned 0 findings each, so the exposure is stale lineage, not leaked credentials.","acceptance_criteria":"From a fresh clone of dexdat/SpecLang, the peeled refs reported by git ls-remote --tags origin for v0.1.0 and v1.0.0 must each pass git merge-base --is-ancestor <peel> origin/main, OR the record must state that a new main-lineage release tag supersedes them and cite the divergence table. No existing tag may be deleted or force-moved to achieve this.","detail":{"metadata":{},"fingerprint":"bfc21f85295156bd307a94361d2ca3107a8212460cb70f42a4ed078127d0b3d9"},"capability_tags":["releng","tag-integrity"],